bodyText:
We might consider implementing some kind of sorting mechanism if we're using a hash of requestID and message.id and we're expecting some duplicates... we only want to "count" the first time we see the duplicate and ideally we're processing these in order from oldest (first occurrence) to newest (last occurrence). But if we're using glob and processing these alphabetically by a randomly assigned UUID, we might want to look at something like first timestamp in the files to make sure we process them in order.
I don't know for sure, but it seems like order matters if we're filtering out duplicates and hoping to keep the session aggregation as accurate as possible.
AI summary and suggestion -
Deduplication Fix Summary
Original Issue: Duplicate Message Counting
Problem Description
When using Claude Code with branched conversations (editing and retrying messages), the usage tracking system was counting the same messages multiple times. This occurred because:

Claude creates branches in conversations when users edit and retry messages
Each branch contains the same message with identical message.id and requestId
The usage analysis tool was counting these duplicate entries, leading to inflated token counts and costs

Original Fix (Commit 36fd76d)
Author: ryoppippi
Date: Sat Jun 7 21:54:04 2025 +0100
Commit: fix: resolve duplicate message id
Core Implementation

Extended Schema: Added optional requestId and message.id fields to the UsageDataSchema
Deduplication Logic: Implemented createUniqueHash() function that creates unique identifiers from message ID + request ID combinations
Global Deduplication: Added deduplication across all files in both loadDailyUsageData() and loadSessionData() functions
Backward Compatibility: Entries without IDs are processed normally (no breaking changes)

Key Features

Prevents Double-Counting: Skips duplicate message+request ID combinations globally across all conversation branches
Handles Edge Cases: Processes entries without IDs normally, allows same message ID with different request IDs
Session Integrity: Ensures session entries are created even if all messages are deduplicated (prevents missing sessions)
Cost Accuracy: Only calculates costs for unique messages, preventing inflated usage numbers in branched conversations

Enhancement: Chronological File Processing
Problem Discovered
While the original fix correctly identified and skipped duplicates, it had a subtle issue with which duplicate to keep:

File Processing Order: Claude stores session data in files named with UUIDs (e.g., 9cea3b20-090d-49a1-a7d2-3aaaa0ad22b4.jsonl)
UUID Alphabetical Sorting: The glob pattern returns files sorted alphabetically by UUID, not chronologically
Cross-Session Duplicates: When the same message appears in multiple sessions (different UUID files), the deduplication would keep whichever file was processed first alphabetically, not the chronologically oldest entry

Example Scenario
File: a1234567-...jsonl (created Jan 5) - Contains msg123
File: f1234567-...jsonl (created Jan 1) - Contains msg123 (same message)

Alphabetical processing order:
1. a1234567 (Jan 5) - msg123 kept ✗
2. f1234567 (Jan 1) - msg123 skipped

Result: Newer entry kept instead of older!

Why This Matters

Accurate Historical Reporting: Session reports should show when tokens were first consumed
Consistent Token Attribution: The first time a message is processed is when tokens are actually used
Correct "Last Activity" Dates: Sessions should reflect accurate timestamps

Solution Implemented
New Function: sortFilesByFirstTimestamp()
async function sortFilesByFirstTimestamp(files: string[]): Promise<string[]> {
  // Read first line of each file to extract timestamp
  // Sort files by timestamp (oldest first)
  // Append files without valid timestamps at end
}
Integration Points

loadDailyUsageData(): Added const sortedFiles = await sortFilesByFirstTimestamp(files);
loadSessionData(): Added const sortedFiles = await sortFilesByFirstTimestamp(files);
loadMonthlyUsageData(): Automatically benefits since it calls loadDailyUsageData()

Technical Details
How It Works

Timestamp Extraction: Reads the first line of each JSONL file to get its earliest timestamp
Chronological Sorting: Sorts all files by their first timestamp (oldest to newest)
Fallback Handling: Files without valid timestamps are appended at the end
Preserves Deduplication: The existing deduplication logic remains unchanged, but now processes files in chronological order

Performance Considerations

Trade-off: Slight performance impact from reading first line of all files
Benefit: Ensures correctness of deduplication across all scenarios
Optimization: Only reads first line, not entire file content

Backward Compatibility

Handles both old schema (timestamp at root) and new schema (message.timestamp)
Files without timestamps are still processed (appended at end)
No changes to output format or API

Testing
Added comprehensive tests to verify:

Files are processed in chronological order regardless of UUID
Oldest entries are preserved when duplicates exist across sessions
Session aggregation remains correct with the new sorting

Summary
This enhancement ensures that the deduplication fix not only prevents double-counting but also preserves the chronologically accurate data. When duplicate messages exist across different sessions, the system now correctly keeps the first occurrence based on actual timestamps, not file processing order.
Recommended Code Changes
1. Add the sortFilesByFirstTimestamp function to src/data-loader.ts
Add this function after the createUniqueHash function (around line 99):
/**
 * Sort files by their first timestamp to ensure chronological processing
 * This ensures deduplication keeps the oldest entries
 */
async function sortFilesByFirstTimestamp(files: string[]): Promise<string[]> {
	const filesWithTimestamps = await Promise.all(
		files.map(async (file) => {
			try {
				const content = await readFile(file, "utf-8");
				const firstLine = content.trim().split("\n")[0];
				if (!firstLine) return { file, timestamp: null };

				const parsed = JSON.parse(firstLine);
				// Handle both old and new schema
				const timestamp = parsed.timestamp || parsed.message?.timestamp;
				return { file, timestamp: timestamp ? new Date(timestamp) : null };
			} catch (e) {
				return { file, timestamp: null };
			}
		}),
	);

	// Filter out files without timestamps and sort by timestamp
	return filesWithTimestamps
		.filter((item) => item.timestamp !== null)
		.sort((a, b) => {
			// Sort by timestamp, oldest first
			// biome-ignore lint/style/noNonNullAssertion: filtered above
			return a.timestamp!.getTime() - b.timestamp!.getTime();
		})
		.map((item) => item.file)
		.concat(
			// Append files without valid timestamps at the end
			filesWithTimestamps
				.filter((item) => item.timestamp === null)
				.map((item) => item.file),
		);
}
